# M Atlas: build prompt 1 (two volumes, shared shell, EN / MN)

You are building **M Atlas**, a small illustrated field guide in two volumes, in English and Mongolian.

- **Vol. I: Birds.** Two real 3D specimens, a Hoopoe and a Kingfisher (source design: Appendix A, "Feather Atlas").
- **Vol. II: Myths.** Two real 3D specimens, a Phoenix and a Kraken (source design: Appendix B, "Myth Atlas").

This prompt has two parts:

1. **The master layer** (this part): what the site is, how the pages connect, what changes from the source designs, the data, the languages, and how Mongolian must behave.
2. **Two source designs** (Appendix A and B), pasted verbatim. They define every pixel, timing, easing, colour, 3D value and interaction of the two viewer pages.

A third source design, a big footer, comes in the next prompt. Until then the content pages use the slim footer described below.

## How to read the appendices

- The appendices are law for **layout, sizes, timing, easing, colours, the 3D scene, interactions and responsive behaviour**. Do not "improve" them. Do not change an easing, a duration, a size or a breakpoint.
- This master layer wins only where it says so, and only for these things: brand, copy, data, language, routes, navigation, fonts for Cyrillic, how the code is organised, and the places listed under "Overrides" below.
- Ignore the sentences in the appendices about "a single standalone file", "no build step", "an import map", "paste the sprite after `<body>`", the `<title>` and meta description, and the `DINOS` array. They described how the templates were exported. Here the site is a multi-route app (see "Stack"). The rule "do not download any asset; load everything from the URLs given" stays: never fetch, copy or re-host an asset while building.
- Every visible word comes from the i18n files (see "Language switching"), including the strings the appendices write in English.

## Stack and code quality

- React 19 + Vite + plain JavaScript (`.jsx` / `.js`; only `server.ts` is TypeScript), `react-router-dom`, an Express server (`server.ts`), `node:sqlite` locally and plain PostgreSQL (`pg`) through `DATABASE_URL` in production. No Firebase or other backend-as-a-service (see "Database").
- Three.js comes from npm, pinned to the exact version of the appendices: `"three": "0.170.0"`. Import `GLTFLoader`, `DRACOLoader`, `RoomEnvironment` from `three/examples/jsm/...` and the Meshopt decoder from `three/examples/jsm/libs/meshopt_decoder.module.js`. The Draco decoder path stays `https://www.gstatic.com/draco/versioned/decoders/1.5.7/`.
- The code must read like it was written by a careful human engineer who will hand it to a full-stack team: clear camelCase names, one responsibility per file, short comments only where the reason is not obvious, no leftover prompt wording, no dead code, no console noise.
- No secrets in the client. Every setting comes from environment variables and is listed in `.env.example`.

