## One engine, two volumes

Appendix A and Appendix B describe the same machine: a 100dvh grid (sidebar, centre stage, info column), one full-hero WebGL canvas, the same rail, turn pill, tabs, zoom, drag, inertia, focus view, dusk light, tab-switch animation and phone layout. Build **one** viewer, `createAtlasViewer(root, { volume, specimens, locale, strings, onNavigate })`, that returns `{ destroy, setLocale, select, setLight }`, mounted by a thin React component. Keep it imperative: the animation loop, the maths and every number stay exactly as in the appendices. A `data-volume="birds"` or `data-volume="myths"` attribute on `.hero` selects the skin. Where the appendices differ, branch on the skin as follows; where they do not, share the code.

| Aspect | `birds` (Appendix A) | `myths` (Appendix B) |
|---|---|---|
| Fonts | Newsreader + Nunito Sans | Fraunces + Instrument Sans + JetBrains Mono |
| Palette | A's day and dusk tokens | B's day and dusk tokens |
| Radii and tab style | A (rounded tabs, tinted selected tab, pill turn control, 6px card) | B (3px radii, specimen number on the tab, accent bar, ink border when selected) |
| Stage | A | B, plus the four plate corner brackets |
| Detail card | A, plain | B, with the double museum-plate rule |
| Headings in the card | "Key Traits", "Habitat" | "Field notes", "Haunts" |
| Sidebar list and feature card | A's | B's (with its 3px accent edge on the feature card) |

**Data parity.** One dataset (see "Data") feeds both skins, so every specimen has every field. The fields that only Appendix B shows (specimen number, first-recorded timeline, scale bar) are also shown in the birds skin, drawn in that skin's own language:

- Eyebrow: `{FAMILY} · {Specimen} {no}` in A's eyebrow style (for example `UPUPIDAE · SPECIMEN MA-101`).
- First-recorded timeline: B's era bar (section 4.4 of Appendix B: same geometry, same `-800 to 1900` span, same segments), coloured with the birds tokens, labels in Nunito Sans 9.5px. It sits after the description, margin-top 14px, exactly where B places it.
- Scale bar: B's (left 0, bottom 30px, 72 × 6 px bar), label in Nunito Sans 10px, tracking .08em.

## Routes

| Route | Page | Footer |
|---|---|---|
| `/` | Vol. I viewer (birds). `?specimen=hoopoe` or `?specimen=kingfisher` selects a tab (default: Kingfisher, as in Appendix A) | none (the viewer is 100dvh) |
| `/myths` | Vol. II viewer (myths). `?specimen=phoenix` or `?specimen=kraken` (default: Phoenix, as in Appendix B) | none |
| `/notes`, `/notes/:slug` | Field notes list and article | slim footer |
| `/about`, `/contact` | scrolling pages | slim footer |
| `/collections/:slug` | one template page for the sidebar categories that have no specimen yet | slim footer |
| `/legal/:slug` (`privacy`, `terms`, `cookies`) | placeholder legal text | slim footer |
| `*` | not found | slim footer |

- Selecting a tab updates `?specimen=` with `history.replaceState`, so the address can be shared.
- Route changes use a paper sheet in the destination page's `--bg` that grows over the screen with `clip-path` (about 600 ms, `cubic-bezier(.76,0,.18,1)`) and then retreats. With `prefers-reduced-motion` the swap is instant.
- A viewer page destroys its renderer on unmount (`renderer.dispose()`, `forceContextLoss()`, dispose every geometry, material and texture, remove listeners, cancel the animation frame), so moving between volumes never keeps two WebGL contexts alive. Cache nothing in memory between visits; the browser's HTTP cache keeps the downloaded files (see "Asset quality").
- Only the active volume's models are ever loaded. The other volume's models load only when that route is visited.

